use std::effects::exception::{ Exception }

## Ability for types that participate in `<-` do-bind syntax.
##
## Implementing `Tryable` lets a type be unwrapped via `<-` inside a `do`
## block. The `try` method extracts the success value or throws via
## `Exception.throw` on failure. The `<-` desugar wraps the surrounding
## block in an implicit `perform ExceptionToResult` (injected by the
## typechecker, not user-visible) so the throw becomes an `Err` in the
## resulting `Result`.
##
## Standard impls live in `std::result` (for `Result 'a 'e`, throws the raw
## error) and `std::option` (for `Option 'a`, throws a synthetic `NoneOpt`
## variant). Custom failure types can implement `Tryable` to participate
## in `<-`.
##
## See `docs/design/do-bind-proposal.md` for the full design.
ability Tryable for 'm having 'inner where {
  ## Extract the success value, throwing on failure.
  ##
  ## On success (`Ok {val}`, `Some {val}`, etc.), return the inner value.
  ## On failure, call `Exception.throw` with whatever value the surrounding
  ## `ExceptionToResult` handler should turn into `Err`.
  ##
  ## Direct calls are unusual — `try` is primarily a desugar target for
  ## `<-`. The receiver's evaluation can have any effect; those effects
  ## flow into the surrounding expression's effect row independently of
  ## `try`'s `<Exception>`.
  try: { it: 'm } -> 'inner <Exception>
}
